WEBVTT

1
00:00:08.931 --> 00:00:11.992
warm wind blew as the last train left the station.

2
00:00:13.593 --> 00:00:19.835
The dog, who had been waiting at the station since three o'clock, searched through the crowd of late-night

3
00:00:19.875 --> 00:00:22.096
travelers for his owner's familiar face.

4
00:00:23.477 --> 00:00:24.757
Where was his master?

5
00:00:25.438 --> 00:00:30.740
The dog's master was never late, but tonight it seemed as though he wasn't coming home at all.

6
00:00:32.328 --> 00:00:37.892
Confused, hungry, and tired, the dog curled up in a corner of the station to wait for the

7
00:00:37.952 --> 00:00:38.772
morning trains.

8
00:00:41.034 --> 00:00:44.676
Hachi was born in northern Japan in November 1923.

9
00:00:46.177 --> 00:00:50.180
He was an Akita, a breed known for its loyalty and intelligence.

10
00:00:51.101 --> 00:00:54.963
Hachi was cream-colored, with pointy ears and an upturned tail.

11
00:00:56.944 --> 00:01:02.108
A few months after his birth, Hachi had been sent to Tokyo to live with Professor Ueno.

12
00:01:03.656 --> 00:01:06.438
Hachi and the professor had immediately become friends.

13
00:01:07.639 --> 00:01:11.882
Each morning Hachi accompanied Professor Ueno to Shibuya Station.

14
00:01:14.104 --> 00:01:18.287
The professor would board the train and go to teach at Tokyo Imperial University.

15
00:01:19.468 --> 00:01:23.951
The last thing the professor saw as he got on the train was Hachi's wagging tail.

16
00:01:25.072 --> 00:01:29.916
The first thing the professor saw upon returning from work was Hachi welcoming him back.

17
00:01:31.228 --> 00:01:34.291
Hachi waited for the professor at the station every day.

18
00:01:35.612 --> 00:01:39.876
He had his own special spot from which he searched the crowds for his dear friend.

19
00:01:42.217 --> 00:01:45.500
Then Professor Ueno and Hachi would walk home together.

20
00:01:46.801 --> 00:01:51.165
This daily routine continued for over a year until tragedy struck.

21
00:01:52.927 --> 00:01:58.952
One morning in 1925, Professor Ueno went to work as usual but didn't return home.

22
00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:04.563
His heart failed, and he died suddenly at the university.

23
00:02:08.326 --> 00:02:13.611
Even though Hachi waited eagerly for his friend at the train station, the professor did not appear.

24
00:02:17.254 --> 00:02:22.498
The stationmaster tried to tell Hachi to go home, but there was no way to explain to the

25
00:02:22.518 --> 00:02:25.100
dog that his owner was never coming back.

26
00:02:26.121 --> 00:02:29.024
The next morning Hachi waited at Shibuya Station.

27
00:02:30.484 --> 00:02:33.347
He continued to wait for weeks, and then months.

28
00:02:35.148 --> 00:02:39.191
The stationmaster felt sorry for Hachi and gave him food and water.

29
00:02:40.432 --> 00:02:43.675
Professor Ueno's relatives came and took Hachi to their home.

30
00:02:44.856 --> 00:02:49.220
Nevertheless, the dog always found his way back to Shibuya Station.

31
00:02:51.101 --> 00:02:57.947
Every day, even in rain and snow, Hachi kept waiting, watching the trains, and hoping for his dear

32
00:02:58.027 --> 00:02:58.848
friend to appear.

33
00:02:59.916 --> 00:03:03.819
For the next ten years, Hachi continued showing up at the station.

34
00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:07.481
He sat at the exact same spot each day.

35
00:03:08.502 --> 00:03:16.227
Even though Hachi never saw the professor's face again, he always remembered where he was happiest: at Shibuya

36
00:03:16.267 --> 00:03:18.869
Station, waiting for his owner to arrive.

37
00:03:21.011 --> 00:03:27.595
Professor Ueno and Hachi had been together for only a short time, but the bond between them was

38
00:03:27.795 --> 00:03:34.377
unshakable. For people passing through Shibuya Station, Hachi became a familiar sight.

39
00:03:35.678 --> 00:03:37.158
They gave him food and water.

40
00:03:38.278 --> 00:03:42.700
Hachi allowed people to pet him, but his attention stayed focused on the trains.

41
00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:49.161
Then, in 1932, a newspaper printed a story about Hachi's loyalty.

42
00:03:50.222 --> 00:03:56.684
After that, people began adding "ko" to his name, which is a friendly, affectionate way of creating a

43
00:03:56.744 --> 00:03:57.744
nickname for a male.

44
00:03:58.996 --> 00:04:01.237
His story touched the hearts of many people.

45
00:04:02.237 --> 00:04:07.460
Soon people from all over Japan began visiting Shibuya Station to see Hachiko.

46
00:04:09.601 --> 00:04:12.402
They wanted to see this amazingly loyal dog.

47
00:04:13.602 --> 00:04:17.024
In March 1935 Hachiko's waiting ended.

48
00:04:17.744 --> 00:04:20.685
He died at his waiting spot at Shibuya Station.

49
00:04:23.326 --> 00:04:26.828
A famous Japanese artist had made a bronze statue of Hachiko.

50
00:04:27.976 --> 00:04:33.401
It was placed at the entrance of Shibuya Station, near the place where Hachiko last saw his dear

51
00:04:33.481 --> 00:04:39.507
friend. Unfortunately, during World War II, the statue was destroyed.

52
00:04:39.847 --> 00:04:41.589
But Hachiko was not forgotten.

53
00:04:43.651 --> 00:04:47.454
The original artist's son later made a second statue of the faithful pet.

54
00:04:48.555 --> 00:04:53.440
The bronze dog continues to wait at Shibuya Station today, looking toward the trains.

55
00:04:55.228 --> 00:04:59.389
Every spring there is a festival to remember Hachiko and his loyalty.

56
00:05:00.410 --> 00:05:03.310
People bring flowers to place at the feet of his statue.

57
00:05:04.251 --> 00:05:08.332
They hear his story and remember what a special dog Hachiko was.

58
00:05:09.732 --> 00:05:12.933
Shibuya Station is now one of the busiest areas in Tokyo.

59
00:05:14.754 --> 00:05:18.615
Hachiko's statue serves as a place for people to meet or wait for others.

60
00:05:19.735 --> 00:05:23.056
Some people pause and reflect on the meaning of Hachiko's story.

61
00:05:24.617 --> 00:05:32.316
In a hectic world, Hachiko represents what is truly most important in our lives: love and loyalty to

62
00:05:32.356 --> 00:05:33.719
those who are dearest to us.
